tool_args.new_string:
\begin{abstract}
Training-free LLM information extraction (IE) enables rapid deployment without labeled data, but performance degrades sharply as prompt schema complexity grows. Real-world ontologies routinely define 50--200+ types, placing them in the regime where extraction becomes unreliable. Prior decomposition strategies conflate two factors: having more types (count) and having semantically similar types (confusability). We present the first controlled study isolating these factors in zero-shot LLM IE, spanning named entity recognition (NER), relation extraction (RE), and event detection across four datasets using Qwen3-32B, with cross-model validation on LLaMA-3.1 (8B, 70B) and Gemini 2.5 Flash. We operationalize confusability via the Schema Confusability Score (SCS, pairwise embedding cosine similarity) and its batch-level aggregation (BCS). Degradation operates along two dimensions: count-related recall decline (up to $-$49\,pp for document-level RE) dominates confusability-related type substitution (${\approx}\,2.2$\,pp in typical batches); at batch level, BCS explains 22--53\% of cross-batch accuracy variance in RE but not in NER, where boundary detection failures overwhelm the type confusion signal. Even few-shot demonstrations do not reduce the confusability gap (+4.3\,pp penalty under 5-shot). Schema decomposition fails in both tasks due to merge amplification (NER) and routing failure (RE), pointing to structural barriers rather than engineering gaps.
\end{abstract}

